    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A kingdom at the Shashe–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> confluence whose rulers moved onto <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapungubwe-hill">Mapungubwe Hill</a> around 1220, apart from the commoners living below.</li><li>It grew rich on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> and gold traded to the coast, and buried its elite with gold, including the famous gold-foil rhinoceros.</li><li>Abandoned around 1290, it passed leadership north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>; today it is a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco">UNESCO</a> site and a national symbol of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The golden rhinoceros of Mapungubwe, small enough to stand in the palm of a hand, was made of thin gold sheet fixed with tiny gold tacks onto a carved wooden core that has since decayed.</p></aside>
    </section>

