    </section>


    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/mapungubwe-hill"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mapungubwe-hill.jpg" alt="Mapungubwe Hill" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/mapungubwe-hill">Mapungubwe Hill</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mapungubwe,_Limpopo,_South_Africa_(20535429052).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">South African Tourism from South Africa, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The Kingdom of Mapungubwe arose in the valley where the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shashe-river">Shashe River</a> joins the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>, today the meeting point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a>. The floodplain, fertile in good years, rich in elephants and well placed for routes to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> coast, had drawn farmers for centuries. From about 900, people of the Zhizo culture, based at Schroda, traded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a> with coastal merchants. In the early 11th century the Zhizo moved west as newcomers of the Leopard’s <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kopje">Kopje</a> tradition, probably speakers of an early form of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a>, arrived and built a large settlement at the site known as K2 or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambandyanalo">Bambandyanalo</a>. A wetter climate favoured farming, and K2 grew rich in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a> and in glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> obtained for ivory and gold, until perhaps 1,500 people lived there. As wealth accumulated, the gap between leaders and commoners widened. Around 1220, during a drier spell, the ruling family moved onto the flat summit of nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapungubwe-hill">Mapungubwe Hill</a>, while their subjects settled in the valley below. Archaeologists see this physical separation as the birth of a kingdom with social classes and sacred leadership, long regarded as the first in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>. Older radiocarbon chronologies dated the hilltop capital to about 1075–1220, but the revised chronology of Thomas Huffman, followed by the University of Pretoria, places it between about 1220 and 1290.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sofala"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sofala.jpg" alt="Sofala" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sofala1683.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Mallet, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mapungubwe’s economy rested on farming and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>. Floods of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> renewed the soils of the valley, where farmers grew <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, pearl and finger <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, cowpeas and ground beans, and cattle, the measure of a man’s wealth and the usual form of marriage payments, were herded out to graze on the lands of allied communities. Hunting, above all of elephants for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivory-trade">ivory</a>, and the working of iron, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and gold supported a busy craft economy, and spindle whorls show that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> was spun and woven into cloth. Long-distance trade added wealth. Ivory and gold were carried down the Limpopo valley to coastal ports, first Chibuene and later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>, and in return came great quantities of glass <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> and South-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-asia">East Asia</a>, as well as Chinese celadon, which has been found at the royal residence. Local craftsmen melted imported beads into large ‘garden roller’ beads. Society was divided into classes. By about 1250 some 5,000 people lived around the capital; the elite occupied the hilltop and were buried there with gold, while commoners lived and were buried in the valley. Only men of high status appear to have worked gold, a metal that at Mapungubwe became a symbol of power and status rather than simply a commodity for export.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>No written source describes Mapungubwe’s wars, and the archaeological evidence for warfare is slight. The choice of a capital on a steep sandstone hill, reached only by a few narrow paths, gave the royal family a naturally defensible seat, and a wooden palisade is thought to have run around it. Stone walls on and around the hill marked off royal space and may also have served for defence. Archaeologists disagree about how much the kingdom depended on force. Some, among them Nam Kim, Chapurukha Kusimba and Simon Hall, argue that coercion and violence must have been needed to hold together a state of this size, while others stress that Mapungubwe’s authority rested on wealth in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cattle">cattle</a>, control of trade and the ritual power of a king believed to bring rain. Relations between the newcomers at K2 and the earlier Zhizo communities, who moved west into what is now <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/botswana">Botswana</a>, may have been hostile, but this too is debated. Iron arrowheads were made locally, along with axes, adzes and chisels. Lesser hilltop sites at some distance from the capital were probably the seats of subordinate chiefs, through whom the king could call on men and labour, but no battle or campaign of the kingdom is known.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mapungubwe is regarded as one of the earliest examples in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a>, in which the ruler lived apart from his people and was linked to the rain on which farming depended. Rainmaking, once practised at several hills in the area and sometimes entrusted to specialists such as San ritual experts, appears to have been concentrated on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mapungubwe-hill">Mapungubwe Hill</a> itself, making the royal family the guardians of the rain. Thomas Huffman’s reading of the hilltop places the first royal palace on its western side, with a room where visitors were received and another where they were screened, and a later palace in the middle of the hill, where visitors addressed the king only through an intermediary. The king was probably surrounded by councillors, praise singers and musicians, while royal wives lived in their own quarter and important men kept large homes on the edge of the town. Beyond the capital the kingdom was organised in tiers, from family heads and village headmen to chiefs ruling from smaller hilltop centres across the basin, who paid tribute in crops, livestock and rarer goods. The state may have covered some 30,000 square kilometres. Some Venda traditions name kings who ruled from the hill, but these accounts are disputed.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe.jpg" alt="Great Zimbabwe" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Conical_Tower_-_Great_Enclosure_III_(33736918448).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Andrew Moore from Johannesburg, South Africa, CC BY-SA 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mapungubwe set the pattern for the states that followed it on the plateau north of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a>. The separation of a sacred ruler from his subjects and the use of stone walls to mark royal space reappeared at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a> and later at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/khami">Khami</a> and other centres of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwe">Zimbabwe</a> tradition. Mapungubwe also shows how early the interior of southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a> was linked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> economy, a connection that brought Indian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bead">beads</a> and Chinese celadon to the Limpopo centuries before Europeans arrived. Recent excavations at Mapela Hill in south-western Zimbabwe, where a hilltop elite may have lived up to two centuries earlier, have reopened the question of where the first such state arose. Mapungubwe was long remembered locally as a sacred hill that should not be climbed. Around New Year 1933 a local guide led the farmer E.S.J. van Graan, his son and others up the hill, where they found gold objects, and the University of Pretoria began excavating in 1933. For decades the finds received little public attention. Since the end of apartheid Mapungubwe has become a national symbol: the Order of Mapungubwe, instituted in 2002, is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-africa">South Africa</a>’s highest honour and its badge shows the golden rhinoceros, and the Mapungubwe Cultural Landscape became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 2003.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>Mapungubwe’s time as a capital was short. Around 1290, or soon after, the hilltop was abandoned and the population of the Shashe–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> basin dispersed to the north-west and south, and the valley never again became the seat of a major state. The causes are still debated. Climate studies once linked the end of the kingdom to a cooler and drier phase in which floodplain farming could no longer feed a large population, but many archaeologists now doubt that climate was the main cause. Shadreck Chirikure and his colleagues stress economic and political change: as coastal merchants trading through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sofala">Sofala</a>, then under the influence of Kilwa, reached the goldfields of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zimbabwean-plateau">Zimbabwe plateau</a> more directly, trade bypassed the Limpopo, and the flow of imported goods with which the king rewarded allies dried up. Leadership and trade passed north to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>, which had grown up on the edge of Mapungubwe’s sphere and adopted its separation of rulers and commoners, its stone walling and its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sacred-kingship">sacred kingship</a>. The people of the basin are thought to be ancestors of the Kalanga, a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shona">Shona</a> group; Venda clans also claim the site, though the evidence for either link is debated. In 2007 the human remains excavated in the 1930s were reburied on the hill.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Kingdom of Mapungubwe, ruled from a hilltop above the Shashe–<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/limpopo">Limpopo</a> confluence between about 1220 and 1290, is widely regarded as southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/africa">Africa</a>’s first class-based state with a sacred king; its gold, its trade with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indian-ocean">Indian Ocean</a> and its royal seclusion set the pattern later followed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-zimbabwe">Great Zimbabwe</a>.</em></p>

